    "register_notes": "Critical essay opening ở C2 Japanese requires balanced deployment của 3-element opening structure. (1) PROBLEM-SETTING (問題提起) — first paragraph identifies what question paper addresses. Best practice: from textual detail, không from abstract proposition. Cụm openers: 〜の冒頭部における〜という細部は、いかなる意味を担うか (what meaning does the detail of〜in the opening of〜carry?), 〜という事実は、〜という問いを必然的に喚起する (the fact of〜necessarily evokes the question of〜). (2) PRIOR RESEARCH ENGAGEMENT (先行研究の確認と差別化) — second paragraph reconstructs received view và differentiates paper. Standard syntax: 〜については、これまで〜と論じられてきた。しかし、〜の点については、十分な検討がなされてこなかった. Critical: reconstruction must be accurate (straw-man kills credibility); differentiation must be specific (vague 'this paper takes a new approach' insufficient). (3) THEORETICAL/METHODOLOGICAL STANCE (理論的・方法論的立場) — third paragraph announces approach. Best practice: theory chosen because demanded by problem, không pre-selected. Cụm: 本研究は、〜という問いに対し、〜の視座から接近する (this study approaches the question of〜from the standpoint of〜). Stylistic principles. (1) BREVITY — opening 3-4 paragraphs maximum. Beyond that, reader loses interest. (2) CONCRETENESS — every claim ở opening should reference specific text, specific scholar, specific concept. Vague rhetoric kills opening. (3) GENERATIVE QUESTION — opening question should be productively answerable, không rhetorical. Question that admits 'yes/no' answer poor; question requiring extended analysis to address effective. (4) SIGNAL STAKES — opening should answer 'why does this question matter?' implicitly. Reader needs sense paper's intellectual contribution justifies attention. Forbidden moves at opening: 'literature is important' (too generic); 'X is a great writer' (canonical assumption); 'in this paper, I will discuss X' (announce-mode without engagement); long autobiographical preamble; quotation without commentary (epigraph alone insufficient). Required: specific, generative, stakes-clear, brief. Critic openings to study (read 5-10 published essays' openings as models): 蓮實重彦's surface-detail openings; 柄谷行人's hypothetical-conditional openings ('もし〜ならば'); 加藤典洋's question-driven openings; 内田樹's contemporary-relevance openings (popular criticism). Different masters, different methods — eclectic learning. Common Japanese academic essay structures vary by venue. 学術論文 (academic paper, journals) more structured. 評論 (critical essay, magazines) more flexible. 書評 (book review) follows specific protocol. Critic adjusts opening to venue.",
